{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DESIGN DECISION (operator ruling this session): FULL READ-WRITE tracker.\n\nOf the three options put to the operator — read-only tracker + certified ops, full read-write, or\ncertified-ops-only with no store — the ruling is FULL READ-WRITE. That resolves open questions\n1 and 3 in the description: the server DOES serve the shared `tickets` branch, and it is a\nWRITER, not read-only. `REBAR_MCP_READONLY` stays unset.\n\nOpen question 2 (dedicated volume vs sharing `reviewbot-tickets`) is resolved as a DEDICATED\nvolume, `/var/gerrit/site/mcp-tickets`. Sharing the review-bot's store would put two independent\nwriters behind one directory; rebar's write lock would serialize them, but the review-bot's store\nwas provisioned for one writer and nothing in its design anticipates a second. A dedicated clone\ncosts one directory and keeps the blast radius of an mcp-side store fault off the review-bot.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION SHAPE (mirrors the review-bot precedent rather than inventing one)\n- Dockerfile.mcp gains an entrypoint that (a) configures a git credential helper from\n  MCP_TICKETS_PAT, (b) clones --single-branch --branch tickets into REBAR_TRACKER_DIR when\n  absent, (c) converges it via infra/scripts/reviewbot-ensure-tickets.sh, (d) exec \"$@\".\n  That script is already generic — it takes the target dir as $1 — so it is REUSED, not copied.\n  infra/ is not in .dockerignore, so it is present in the mcp build context.\n- docker-compose.yml: dedicated external volume + REBAR_TRACKER_DIR + MCP_TICKETS_PAT.\n- compose-up.sh: extend SITE_SUBDIRS with `mcp-tickets`. This is REQUIRED, not optional —\n  config-check.sh check 5 diffs compose's external volumes against --print-volumes, so a compose\n  volume added without extending that list cannot reach main.\n- fetch-secrets.sh: materialize MCP_TICKETS_PAT from SSM; terraform ssm.tf declares the param.\n- autodeploy.sh mcp_run_new: mount the volume and pass REBAR_TRACKER_DIR, so blue-green\n  containers get the same store as the compose boot container.\n\nFAILURE POSTURE, deliberately SOFT — and this differs from the mcp static-token posture.\nNo PAT => the clone is DEFERRED and the container still boots and serves (the review-bot's\n\"clone deferred (deploy canary)\" pattern). It is NOT fail-closed like mcp-static-tokens.json,\nbecause an auth secret and a data store are different risks: a missing bearer store must never\nserve an unauthenticated endpoint, whereas a missing ticket clone should degrade to \"tracker\nunavailable\", not take the endpoint down. With bug antelopine-limivorous-chinchilla landed, that\ndegradation is now LOUD — reads raise \"ticket system not initialized\" instead of returning [].\n\nOPERATOR ACTION REQUIRED — this ticket cannot be fully effective without it.\nA GitHub PAT with push rights to the `tickets` branch must be placed in the new SSM SecureString.\nI cannot mint that credential. Until it is populated the clone stays deferred and the store stays\nabsent; the code lands and is inert. Flagging here rather than silently shipping something that\nlooks complete but has never once produced a store on the box.\n\nMERGE-ORDER NOTE for the sibling ticket mobile-groovy-badger (/health store visibility): that\nchange is written to be INERT until a tracker dir is explicitly configured, precisely so it\ncannot take down the currently-storeless production endpoint if it lands first. Once THIS ticket\nsets REBAR_TRACKER_DIR, the health gate becomes strict on its own. Either merge order is safe;\nthat is by construction, not luck."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787713151375139001,"uuid":"e82beddf-f5ce-4cfa-b9e1-8358bac6527d"}